m62.content:
The strongest diagnosis is `com.google.javascript.jscomp.TypeCheck.visitGetProp(com.google.javascript.jscomp.NodeTraversal, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node)`.

Why this method stands out:

- Both failures are missing `GETPROP` warnings.
- `testGetprop4()` expects a null-base property warning for `x.prop = 3`.
- `testIssue810()` expects a missing-property warning for `obj.unknownProp` on the right-hand side of `this.prop = obj.unknownProp`.

The suspicious logic is here in [`TypeCheck`](< /workspace/com.google.javascript.jscomp.TypeCheck >) around lines 1314-1318:

```java
} else if (n.getJSType() != null && parent.isAssign()) {
  return;
} else if (validator.expectNotNullOrUndefined(...)) {
  checkPropertyAccess(...);
}
```

That early return is too broad:

- Type inference assigns a type to `GETPROP` nodes ahead of type checking.
- For any `GETPROP` under an `ASSIGN`, this code can return before running either:
  - the null/undefined base check, or
  - the nonexistent-property check.
- That matches both observed regressions:
  - `x.prop = 3` skips `"No properties on this expression"`.
  - `obj.unknownProp` on the RHS of an assignment can skip `"Property unknownProp never defined on obj"`.

The key defect is that the guard checks only `parent.isAssign()`, not whether `n` is specifically the left-hand side of that assignment. The method is likely suppressing warnings for all property accesses inside assignments, including RHS accesses that should still be checked.
